  "description": "Learn to ask for prices, bargain, and navigate markets and stores in Chinese.",
  "guidebook": "### Asking for Prices\nTo ask how much something costs in Chinese, use the phrase **多少钱** (*duōshao qián*), which literally translates to \"how much money\". Place the item first:\n- **这个多少钱？** (*Zhège duōshao qián?* — How much is this?)\n\n### Everyday Currency\nWhile the official currency unit is the *yuán* (元), people almost always say **块** (*kuài*) in daily conversation when shopping:\n- **十块钱** (*shí kuài qián* — Ten yuan)\n\n### Expressing Extremes\nUse the pattern **太...了** (*tài ... le*) to say something is \"too much\":\n- **太贵了！** (*Tài guì le!* — Too expensive!)\nTo suggest a discount, you can say **便宜一点吧** (*Piányi yìdiǎn ba* — A little cheaper, please).",
